# Project Definition Protocol

This protocol converts a deterministic repository scan and plain-language human input into a canonical document-first bundle. It does not use an LLM to rewrite answers, choose filenames, or promote repository inference into authority. The resulting target documents are ordinary project-owned Markdown and remain useful after setup without a package or continuing runtime.

## Boundary

The protocol records visible evidence; it does not create a task database, hidden journal, scheduler, dispatcher, command runner, Gate, evidence archive, automatic closure, or target workflow runtime. It does not execute project commands, dispatch Agents, create a Plan, or grant permissions. Main and the host/project own those actions separately.

No generated target artifact directs an Agent to locate, install, resolve, import, invoke, or verify Reporivet or a package diagnosis command. No target role or procedure Skill, package marker, generated settings, copied runtime, command wrapper, executor metadata, hooks, or privilege-bearing configuration is part of the generated target surface.

## Evidence states

Every topic is represented with four visibly separate states:

- **Confirmed** — explicit user input or a repository fact that an authorized owner accepts.
- **Proposed** — a scanner observation or candidate interpretation awaiting review.
- **Open** — an unanswered, conflicting, unavailable, or unresolved fact.
- **Sources** — paths, records, or other provenance for the statements above.

A scanner may suggest paths, commands, technologies, owners, or relationships, but it never promotes them to Confirmed. A missing answer remains Open; no value or default is inferred.

## Guided topics

The visible definition draft covers, at minimum:

- identity, purpose, and goals;
- users, workflows, and success;
- system boundary, structure, dependencies, and external systems;
- product requirements and non-goals;
- design, accessibility, and interaction principles;
- quality checks and evidence expectations;
- operations, release, backup, rollback, recovery, and incidents;
- security, permissions, sensitive data, and disclosure; and
- Plan and task-ownership working agreements.

The draft is reviewable Markdown. Resume preserves existing answers and evidence states. Finalization rejects unresolved required fields rather than manufacturing authority.

## Strict static-runbook boundary

A procedure record is eligible for a static runbook only when one complete, unique, user-confirmed strict structured record is present in `Confirmed`. The record contains exactly these nine fields: `slug`, `title`, `trigger`, `reads`, `actions`, `stop_conditions`, `evidence`, `permissions`, and `rollback`. All nine fields are required; no additional fields or aliases are accepted, and no values or defaults are inferred.

A qualifying record renders exactly one ordinary Markdown file at `docs/runbooks/<slug>.md` during resumed setup only; ordinary target work does not depend on package-side context. The file is descriptive and instruction-only project documentation, not an executor. It carries no command execution, Agent spawn/dispatch, privilege-bearing frontmatter, executor metadata, hooks, command registration, shell metadata, tool permissions, or other privilege-bearing configuration.

Generic, inferred, incomplete, Proposed, Open, Sources-only, malformed, or duplicate records never create a runbook; no procedure is inferred or executed. Existing differing, stale, arbitrary, unsafe, symlinked, or nonregular runbooks remain preserved and are never deleted by rendering, or are refused rather than overwritten by inference. A filename, marker, frontmatter block, or location alone never proves ownership.

## Project-owned handoff

An approved handoff may create or preserve the documented Markdown tree and eligible static runbooks, subject to exact ownership and immediate precondition checks. It does not execute project commands, dispatch Agents, create a Plan, or leave hidden state. After handoff, package absence is expected and project commands, CI, deployment, Git, runbooks, Plans, and host-native Agents remain project-owned.

For substantive follow-up work, Main searches active and completed Plan history and resumes exactly one matching active ordinary Markdown Plan or creates the lowest unused current-year Plan from `docs/exec-plans/_template.md` without overwriting an existing file. Broad roots use bounded Task Owners; narrow or inherently serial roots remain direct nondelegating leaves. Verification starts from a fresh context and is candidate-specific, read-only, nonrepairing, and nondelegating.

## Sources and maintenance

Keep source paths next to Confirmed statements. Update this protocol when the evidence states, guided topics, static runbook schema, generated surface, or handoff boundary changes. Link the governing product specification, design, decision, and completed Plan; do not encode implementation details or package operations here.
